  "body": "**What happens.** CamCraft's tool rules refuse a tip angle of 180 degrees or more (`crates/tools/src/tool_sanity.rs`: \"tip angle ... must be under 180\"). A flat-bottom drill (point angle 180, used for flat-bottomed blind holes, drilling on curved or angled faces, and half-hole/intersecting holes) therefore has no place in a tool library: it cannot be created, and an imported library entry for one is refused.\n\n**Evidence of demand.** A third-party converter (Dutchman 0.3.1-rc.7, a library-to-`.camtools` test) drops 48 tools of a real vendor library for this rule alone. Only the count is used here; no library content.\n\n**Expected (general machining practice):**\n- A flat-bottom drill is a drill with a 180-degree point: its tip is a flat disc of the full diameter. Drill cycles (G81/G83 and the like) post it like any drill.\n- Stock simulation removes a flat-bottomed cylinder; a blind hole's floor is flat, with no cone.\n- Hole depth means the flat floor (no tip allowance).\n- Verify sees the flat floor (no cone below the programmed depth).\n\n**Done means:** a drill with a 180-degree point is accepted and round-trips through `.camtools`; drilling a blind hole with it simulates a flat floor and posts the depth without tip allowance, each pinned by a test. Other tip angles still keep their current range. Related: #897, #3573 (other hole-making kinds).\n",
